# GC110 Paraplegia, spinal cord compression; transverse myelitis; spinal dysraphism; neuroimaging III spinal cord

## Clinical approach to paraplegia or spinal cord injury

- Anatomical distribution:
  - Motor - UMN vs LMN
  - Sensory - dorsal column, spinothalamic tract
  - Autonomic - sphincteric functions
- Level of injury by motor distribution –\> rough localisation:
  - C3 or above - breathing and tetraplegia
  - C5 or above - complete tetraplegia (brachial plexus involved)
  - C6 lesion - C5 not involved, allows shoulder abduction (not as functionally limiting)
  - Thoracic-lumbar lesions –\> paraplegia
- Sensory loss - segmental (dermatomal) vs sensory level (ascending tract):
  - Sensory level for precise localisation
- Conus medullaris and cauda equina
- P/E:
  - Neurological
  - DRE - sanal anaesthesia, tone, contraction
  - Bulbocarvenous reflex
- Spinal shock - two meanings:
  - Neurological - flaccid paralysis and areflexia after cord lesions for 1-2 weeks then classical hyperreflexia
  - Hypotension after SCI - neurogenic shock due to vasodilatation and bradycardia (loss of sympathetic drive)
- Extent of injury - complete vs incomplete injury (difference in prognosis):
  - Classification of spinal cord injury - ASIA classification
- Causes of SCI or myelopathy and non-compressive cause (see slides causes of myelopathy p23-24):
  - Most common causes:
    - Spinal trauma - primary vs secondary neurological injury (prevent during instability, haematoma, and iatrogenic during transport)
    - Spinal tumour
    - Degenerative conditions
    - Infection
    - Spinal dysraphism

## Spinal trauma

- Mechanism of spinal injury - stablev vs unstable injury:
  - Vertical compression -\> stable
  - Hinge injury - dependent on intact ligaments
  - Shearing injury -\> unstable
- Ix - X-Ray, CT and MRI spine (T2 cord oedema)
- Principles of Mx:
  - ABC - resuscitation (NB spinal shock)
  - Protect spine - collar and log roll (assume head injury)
  - Imaging - as above + CT brain to r/o head injuries
  - Steroids (Methylprednisolone for cord oedema cf DMX for brain) –\> controversial
  - Stabilisation and rehabilitation
- Stabilisation:
  - Traction
  - +/- Surgical decompression with implants or bone grafts

## Spinal tumours

- Classification by location:
  - Extradural - metastatic tumour, primary brain tumour
  - Intradural extramedullary - meningioma, nerve sheath tumours
  - Intradural intramedullary - astrocytoma, ependymoma (glial cells origins)
- Spinal metastasis:
  - Most commonly from breast, lung, or prostate, and commonly thoracic
  - Route:
    - Haematogenous
    - Direct invasion
    - Lymphatic
    - Subarachnoid seeding from primary and secondary CNS neoplasm
- Intradural extramedullary tumours:
  - Meningioma - typically thoracic, middle-aaged female a/w NF-II
  - Schwannoma - Cervical or lumbar (NF II)
- Intramedullary tumours - NFII in spinal ependymoma in adults, astrocytoma in children
- Principles of Mx:
  - Steroids –\> not as effective as brain tumour
  - Surgical resection with safety limit
  - Radiotherapy
  - For metastasis (palliative) - primary ERT, surgery for intractable pain, instability, or resistent to RT

## Degenerative

- Spondylosis - know pathoanatomy and pathophysiology
- Know Mx - analgesia, physiotherapy, know indications of surgery
- Know acute on chronic presentation - cervical spondylosis asymptomatic, but fall and create central cord syndrome

## Infection

- Source:
  - From adjacent foci - Osteomyelitis, TB spine
  - Haematogenous spread - Epidural abscess
  - ?Spondylodisciitis

## Spinal dysraphism

- Rare
- Cause - due to embryological maldevelopment:
  - Spina bifida cystica - failed neural tube closure (skin also affected), containing CNS or CSF or both:
    - Complications:
      - Rupture - CSF leak, meningitis
      - Neurological - paraplegia and sphincter dysfunction
  - Spina bifida occulta - more subtle than spina fibida cystica
- Tethered cord syndrome –\> common spina bifida occulta:
  - Anchoring of lower end spinal cord by fatty filum terminates, lipoma (read up), cord under tension as spine lengthens as conus medullaris teathered to S2 by filum terminalis
  - Clinical feature:
    - LL LMN deficit
    - sphincter dysfunction
    - scoliosis and foot deformity
- Mx - unteather by surgery (cut filum terminalis)

## Complications

### Neurogenic bladder

### Autonomic dysreflexia

### Contractility, spasticity, pressure sores
